  "source": "CCSDS 504.0-B-2 annex G",
  "note": "Attitude Data Messages. The examples are shipped as readable .kvn files under corpus and carried here as decode vectors, hex-encoded because that is the only input form the schema has. All three messages are covered: the APM, the AEM and the ACM. The ACM vectors are mostly about shape, because its sections are what say how to read its rows — but unlike the ODM's OCM, that shape is self-checking. Annex B4 prints the component count of every ATT_TYPE and RATE_TYPE, annex B6 does the same for every COV_TYPE, and table 5-4 makes NUMBER_STATES mandatory besides, so the vectors carry the types, the declared count and the row width together and hold them to agreeing. One ACM vector is in the XML form of clause 7.7 rather than key-value notation, and reports the same fields. What the vectors assert is which blocks a message carried, the frames a rotation goes between, and for an AEM the attitude type and the resulting line width. The numbers are floats, which a vector field cannot hold, and are checked in pkg/adm against the same published text.",
